          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Iron deficiency anemia sits in the blood chapter. Which chapter would an underlying bleeding ulcer be coded from?",
          "answer": "A bleeding ulcer would be coded from the digestive system chapter, not the blood chapter. This comes from the ICD-10-CM Chapter 11 summary (K00–K95), which places the peptic ulcer categories in the esophagus, stomach and duodenum block. They are divided by site: gastric, duodenal, peptic unspecified and gastrojejunal.\n\nThe blood chapter summary (ICD-10-CM Chapter 3) supports the same idea from the other direction. It says chronic blood loss causing iron deficiency is coded from whichever chapter owns the bleeding site. So the iron deficiency anemia would sit in Chapter 3, and the bleeding ulcer would sit in Chapter 11.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not give the exact code for a bleeding peptic ulcer. They also did not say how to sequence the two conditions together. Check the full coding manual or a certified coder for that.\n\nBleeding from the gut is a warning sign that needs prompt assessment. The gastrointestinal bleeding guidance (gastroenterology and emergency medicine society guidance) says black, tarry stool and any unexplained bleeding should be checked by a clinician rather than assumed to be harmless. If you or someone you know has black stool, vomiting blood, or feels faint or very weak, seek urgent medical care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
